WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.820
The Pleistocene Park idea

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.920
that the Zimovs have been working on for 30 years

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:12.680
is that the animal biomass in the entire Arctic

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:15.960
and subarctic used to be a 100x greater than it is now.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.040
It's basically depauperate now in ecological terms.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:28.500
They're putting a density of grazing animals—
Yakutian horses, which you saw in the first video;

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.820
reindeer; buffalo; they're trying to get
some more American bison there;

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:37.940
musk ox; and so on.
Get them in the density they used to be.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:43.700
They trample the snow which they need to do
to get at the grass in the winter.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:48.180
That gets the cold down through the snow
to the permafrost, keeping it cold.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:54.180
Grazers make grass that's how it works
in Africa; that's how it works everywhere.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:58.640
Mammoths will not be completely required
to get the mammoth steppe back,

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:05.520
but it would help because elephants are good
at ripping up or knocking down trees.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:10.360
That opens it up for other grazers
to come in and turn it into grassland.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:16.080
That's a normal cycle the way it develops
in, say, the Serengeti in Africa.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:21.080
The richness of animal life that you see
in those parts of protected Africa now

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.100
are like it used to be all over the world.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.900
Including here. And including the Arctic and subarctic.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:34.300
One of the reasons there's hope of getting the woolly mammoths back, once we go through this process,

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:38.240
is that it's wonderful habitat for them now

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.740
with the other animals to help turn it into grassland.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:45.540
They are grazers themselves. And
if you get enough of all these animals back

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:49.060
you get the mammoth steppe back
which is grass which fixes carbon.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:52.600
Replacing the tundra which is currently thawing

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.280
and releasing greenhouse gases.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:04.700
This is Eriona Hysolli
from George Church's lab at Harvard.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:10.380
She's looking very calm and studied.
But she's thrilled out of her mind.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:17.700
Because this is a the trunk of a female mammoth

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:24.340
who was 60 years old when she died
some 10 or 12 thousand years+ ago.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:28.620
Because of the cold permafrost and all of that

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:32.360
this is not a fossil; this is the trunk of the animal.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:39.460
In a way the trunk is
the most elephant part of an elephant.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:44.680
To be able to take hold of, wearing the blue gloves,

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:50.680
the trunk of this female mammoth from that long ago

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:56.840
was a personal connection across that time
to how it used to be

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:03.000
and to how it might be again when creatures exactly
like her can come back to that part of the world.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.580
What she is doing is cutting off pieces of tissue

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:10.120
which she got from this and from another museum

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:14.040
and taking it back to Harvard.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.500
This is one of those things where the Russians say, well,

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:20.160
there's a whole lot of rules and you've got to
get this that and the other permissions.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:23.000
And it's basically hopeless.
Don't even think of taking tissue with you.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:28.440
Would you like a plastic bag to carry it in?

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.500
We gradually caught on, that's how things work.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.280
The answer is always "Nyet."

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.460
But there's a way.

